#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8f012d0-444a-5035-8a72-a053de312d63" :knowledge-point/title "Changing the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa2df4a4-9d68-50c5-93b4-4362e410abfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328384} {:answer/id #uuid "6977f932-8bbd-5482-904c-e56c872e0e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328385} {:answer/id #uuid "58fcff74-31d5-58ac-9115-1a0243db96d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328386} {:answer/id #uuid "1968c1ef-acfc-5ef1-b819-6fa362a27198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328387} {:answer/id #uuid "cd859160-7d0d-5fba-a943-39d5596dcb50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328388} {:answer/id #uuid "d74ba33f-b4d1-598e-a9f9-7316815deed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d74ba33f-b4d1-598e-a9f9-7316815deed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328389} :answer-field/id #uuid "249bca12-e7c7-588f-b659-2eefa1ac3328" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80bfbd6a-391f-520f-a0ad-dc736395685b" :question/math-academy-id "q-72175" :question/problem "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le y\\le 2}.$\n\n- ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$\n- ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{1+x^2y^2}$ is continuous on the rectangle $D=[0,\\sqrt2]\\times[-\\sqrt3,2]$. Fubini's theorem gives both I and II directly.\n\nIn III, the intervals are assigned to the opposite coordinate variables. Its region is the reflected rectangle $D^*=[-\\sqrt3,2]\\times[0,\\sqrt2]$, rather than $D$. Nevertheless, the integral has the same value: set $u=y$ and $v=x$, whose absolute Jacobian is $1$. Because $f(v,u)=f(u,v)$,\n\n$$\\int_{-\\sqrt3}^{2}\\int_0^{\\sqrt2}\\sqrt{1+x^2y^2}\\,dy\\,dx\n=\\int_{-\\sqrt3}^{2}\\int_0^{\\sqrt2}\\sqrt{1+u^2v^2}\\,du\\,dv\n=\\iint_D\\sqrt{1+x^2y^2}\\,dA.$$\n\nThus I, II, and III are all true. The original source's answer \"I and II only\" identifies the two expressions whose bounds directly match the stated coordinates, but omits the equal value in III due to symmetry. The study answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}]}
